The '''Kurupiranga''' ({{Literal translation|the people}}), also known as the '''Kurupiran''' are an [[W:Ethnolinguistic group|ethnolinguistic group]] of [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous peoples]] of the north-central [[Abyala]], mostly concentrated in southwestern Balisca ([[Pindorama]]), with significantly smaller communities in northwestern [[Asikyira]]. 

"Kurupiranga" is a modern collective term for the peoples of the region; however, the term was not historically used by the indigenous populations themselves. There was no common sense of identity or political unity among the distinct populations, societies and ethnic groups because they each had their own particular traditions, cultures and historical identity. The collective term refers to a wide-range of the [[Kurupiran-Nyengatú languages|Kurupiran-Nyengatú]] [[W:Linguistic homeland|urheimat]]; as the Illypnians explored the [[Marañón basin]], they found that wherever they went along the vast river, most of the indigenous peoples spoke similar languages. The colonists took advantage of these similarities, systematizing common standards then named [[Kurupiran language|''Kurupirangüe'']] ({{Literal translation|the people's language}}).

Today the collective group makes up over 75% of the [[indigenous peoples in Balisca]], and about 4% of the total Baliscan population. The Kurupiranga are particularly concentrated in the [[Indigenous nations in Balisca|indigenous nation]] of [[Pindorama]] within the confederation. 

